{.exercise ...}
Work, perhaps with a partner:

1.  Download and extract the [skeleton code](files/emptyloop/emptyloop.zip)
    (either on portal or [NX](https://www.cs.virginia.edu/wiki/doku.php?id=nx_lab)
    or on another system with a Unix-like environment and a C compiler).

2.  Look at the function `measure_times_plain` in `empty-time.c` and/or read its
    description below.

3.  Use `make` to build the `empty-time` and `long-loop` programs.

4.  Obtain measurements from `empty-time` for at least 1 million iterations.
    You can do this with a command like `./empty-time plain 1000000 output.csv`.

5.  Plot the times you recorded. I recommend plotting in one or more of the following ways:

    *  with the start time on the x axis and the duration on the y axis, and a log scale for
        the y axis
    *  with a histogram of the durations, using log-scale-based buckets

6.  Based on your plots determine a threshold above which an exception likely interrupted
    the execution of your program.
    Obtain measurements filtering out items below that threshold until you have at least 10000 such
    smaples. You can do this with a command like
    `THRESHOLD=XXX ./empty-time threshold 10000 output.csv` where XXX is your chosen threshold.

7.  Based on your measurements, answer the following questions in a text document:

    *  Do there appear to be exceptions that happen on a regular schedule?
    *  What are the typical handling times for exceptions?
    *  Can you estimate the context switch interval?

8.  If you have time, compare your results above with one or more of the following variations:

    a.  If you originally measured on a non-portal machine, measure on portal or vice-versa
        and compare.  Note that you may need to set a different threshold.
    a.  Run `./empty-time` "pinned" to particular cores. On portal (or other Linux systems),
        you can do this with by running `taskset -c X ./empty-time` instead of `./empty-time`
        where `X` is one of the numbers output by `lscpu --extended=CPU`.
    a.  Run `./empty-time` at the same time as some application that should make substantial
        use of the system. To maximize the change of them interferring, run both applications
        on the same cores using `taskset -c X ...` (where X is a core number).

        You can use the provided `./long-loop` application for this; for example doing something
        like

            taskset -c 3 ./long-loop &
            THRESHOLD=X taskset -c 3 ./empty-time threshold 10000 output.csv


    Answer the following questions in a text document:

    *  Is there a different pattern of exceptions under these variations?
       If so, what characterizes these differences.

9.  Show a TA:

    *  Your plots.

    *  Your answers to the questions in item 7 and 8.

{/}

# `measure_times_plain`

    static void measure_times_plain(int count, long *starts, long *durations) {
        struct timespec first, current, previous;
        clock_gettime(WHICH_CLOCK, &first);
        clock_gettime(WHICH_CLOCK, &previous);
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {
            clock_gettime(WHICH_CLOCK, &current);
            starts[i] = nsec_difference(&previous, &first);
            durations[i] = nsec_difference(&previous, &current);
            previous = current;
        }
    }

This function reads a clock in a loop `count` times, and records the results to the
`starts` and `durations` array.
The only thing that happens between obtaining samples is storing the time and related bookkeeping.

If this loop were the only thing running, we would expect to see the `durations`
are roughly constant. You should

# `measure_times_threhsold`

This function adds the following to `measure_times_plain`

1.  Code to read from the environment variable `THRESHOLD` into the local variable `threshold`:

        const char *threshold_string = getenv("THRESHOLD");
        if (!threshold_string) {
            fprintf(stderr, "THRESHOLD environment variable not set (example: THRESHOLD=100 ./empty-time ...)\n");
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }
        long threshold = atoi(threshold_string);

2.  Code to check the the threshold before recording a value (and corresponding
    adjustments to the loop):

        long difference = nsec_difference(&previous, &current);
        if (difference > threshold) {
            starts[i] = nsec_difference(&previous, &first);
            durations[i] = nsec_difference(&previous, &current);
            i += 1;
        }


